Notebook[{
Cell["Hypermatrix", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"2c0a60f2-ca10-e447-8958-e64594d6b60d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"Hypermatrix", "[", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"arr1", ",", " ", "arr2", ",", " ", "..."}], "}"}], "]"}], 
   "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "00bc99cb-dc0b-af4c-bbb2-bc7054e90eb6"],
 " ",
 "represents the given arrays as a hypermatrix, holding them in canonical \
order."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"8a4424ee-75b1-3040-9011-2b26f3435ff4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"Hypermatrix", "[", 
    RowBox[{"arr1", ",", " ", "arr2", ",", " ", "..."}], "]"}], 
   "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "5bc1f078-f1ea-f14b-8c81-50526e8e3b93"],
 " ",
 "is equivalent."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"defed912-ea83-e34a-8327-5846275f030a"],

Cell["Details and Options", "NotesSection",ExpressionUUID->"77f54916-9aa1-1440-85ac-8633e43b6a74"],

Cell["\<\
The arrays are ordered by array order, lowest first; then by dimensions, \
shorter first, compared term by term; then by symmetry, taking \"Symmetric\" \
before \"Cyclic\" before \"Rigid\".\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"95dcca9c-a001-9a42-ad85-fe174257af42"],

Cell["\<\
Two arrays alike in all three are put in the Wolfram Language's own canonical \
order, so that a hypermatrix is a function of the set of arrays it was given \
and of nothing else. Giving the same arrays in a different order produces the \
identical expression.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"606e1bea-6925-8042-98e4-4012014b113d"],

Cell["\<\
An irregular array is necessarily \"Rigid\", since only a regular array can \
carry a symmetry, so the symmetry criterion separates the regular arrays \
among themselves.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"6107699b-40d6-094d-bfc0-3f757783603e"],

Cell["\<\
Arrays of any kind of entry sit together freely. Raw entries given in place \
of an array are promoted to an ArrayObject.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"e820d34f-5e98-c64d-80f3-c323356b4afb"],

Cell["\<\
Length gives the number of arrays, and Normal the entries of each of them in \
order.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"dc284432-42fb-474f-8251-69c26cd77dd9"],

Cell["Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection",ExpressionUUID->"dd563ce6-203d-8140-a571-3b84413c9b0e"],

Cell["The arrays are sorted whatever order they are given in:", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"9ac86362-79b9-2e48-b237-5d7f049db70d"],

Cell["\<\
hm = Hypermatrix[{
   ArrayObject[Array[1 &, {2, 2, 2}], \"Symmetric\"],
   ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}],
   ArrayObject[{1, 2}],
   ArrayObject[{1, 2, 3}],
   ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {2, 1}}, \"Symmetric\"],
   ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {2, 1}}, \"Cyclic\"]}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"2bfe1737-4304-2842-bf69-53a73f4d54a6"],

